Acoustic Connect for Capacitor

Acoustic Connect plugin for Capacitor. Requires Capacitor 8 and Node 22 or newer.

New Features

- Taps and gestures on page elements, on Android and iOS. A tap on an element
  of the app's page is logged as a click and a tap on that element, and a
  double tap, a long press, a swipe and a pinch as that gesture, as the SDK logs
  them on a native control: with the element's DOM id (or its path from the
  nearest element with one), its kind and its position. The plugin's page
  script listens in the app's main frame only, passively, and reads no text or
  field value. Android needs the Connect Android SDK 11.1.39-beta or later: set
  `androidVersion` to it, since the default 11.1.17 logs nothing. iOS needs the
  Connect iOS SDK 2.2.35 or later, the beta SDK, which the beta npm package links
  by default. The release SDK (the public npm package on Swift Package Manager,
  `useRelease: true` on CocoaPods) or an `iosVersion` below 2.2.35 logs nothing
  on iOS until a release SDK has the functions
- iOS, the Connect iOS SDK build: with Swift Package Manager the npm package
  decides. The beta package links the beta SDK, `ConnectDebug-SP` from 2.2.35;
  the public package the release SDK, `Connect-SP` from 2.1.51. With CocoaPods
  `useRelease` decides, as on Android: by default the `AcousticConnectDebug`
  pod 2.2.35 or newer, with `useRelease: true` the `AcousticConnect` pod 2.1.51
  or newer
- Migration on iOS. An app on Swift Package Manager whose rich-push extensions
  were added with `npx acoustic-connect setup-ios-push` runs that command once
  more after updating the plugin: it moves the extensions to the plugin's
  package, and otherwise Xcode stops on two packages with a `Connect` module.
  A CocoaPods app does the same after updating the plugin or changing
  `useRelease` (it moves the extensions' pod; then `pod install`), and needs the
  Acoustic spec repo as a Podfile source before `pod install`, as the
  `AcousticConnectDebug` versions the plugin takes are published only there:
  run `npx acoustic-connect setup-ios-pods`. `npx acoustic-connect doctor`
  checks both (`ios-connect-package`, `ios-pod-sources`)
- `npx acoustic-connect setup-ios-pods [dir] [--dry-run]` adds the Acoustic
  CocoaPods spec repo, `https://github.com/go-acoustic/PodSpecs.git`, ahead of
  the sources in `ios/App/Podfile`. CocoaPods trunk becomes read-only on
  2 December 2026, and Connect iOS SDK versions released after that are
  published only to that repo. `setup-ios-push` adds the source too,
  `npx acoustic-connect doctor` warns when the Podfile lacks it
  (`ios-pod-sources`), and `pod install` prints a warning until it is there.
  Apps that use Swift Package Manager need nothing

Fixes

- iOS: tapping a push notification that launched a killed app now runs its
  action (open URL, open dialer) in automatic push mode when the SDK is started
  by `enable()` from the page (`autoEnable: false`, or no keys in the plugin
  config). The tap used to be lost: iOS delivered it to Capacitor's notification
  router before the SDK had taken over notification handling. The plugin now
  keeps the tap from launch and replays it once `enable()` has run, as long as
  that happens within 30 seconds of the tap. Capacitor's own notification
  handling still receives the tap meanwhile. Nothing is kept in manual push mode
  or when push is off. An app that starts the SDK from the config was not
  affected
- Android: after a push had started the app's process (a killed app), the SDK
  could not post any event once the page loaded: the posts failed and the queue
  was only sent after the process restarted. Capacitor's cookie handler, which
  the SDK cannot use, stayed in place when the SDK was already running before
  the plugin loaded, and the plugin replaced it only when it set the SDK up
  itself. It now replaces the handler when it loads, whether the app starts the
  SDK from the config, calls `enable()` or only checks `isSdkEnabled()`, so the
  delivered signal and the events that follow reach the collector
- Android: `setCurrentScreenName` now only names the screen that later events
  are attributed to, as on iOS. It used to log a LOAD screen view as well: an
  app that relied on that logs the LOAD with `logScreenViewContextLoad`. Log
  each screen of the page (a tab, a route, a view inside one) with
  `logScreenViewContextLoad` when it is entered and
  `logScreenViewContextUnload` when it is left, the LOAD of the next screen
  first, as the sample apps now do. On Android this needs the Connect Android
  SDK 11.1.12-beta or later; the default, 11.1.17, has it

Known Issues

- A swipe or a pinch that the WebView takes over to scroll or zoom the page is
  not logged, on Android and iOS: the page sees the touch cancelled. A gesture
  on an element that handles it itself (`touch-action: none`) is logged
- On iOS, with the SDK handling push in automatic mode, its notification delegate
  takes the place of Capacitor's notification router, so Capacitor's push and
  local-notification events (such as `pushNotificationActionPerformed`) do not
  reach the app. Use manual push mode to keep them
- A notification arriving and being tapped has not been checked with a real push
  on a real device. Registration is checked on the Android emulator and the iOS
  simulator
- Rich push has not been received on a real device (the iOS simulator does not
  show rich media)
- The manual push calls (`didRegisterWithToken` and the others) are checked with
  a fake SDK, not against a real push
- `iosVersion` and `useRelease` apply on iOS only to apps that use CocoaPods;
  with Swift Package Manager the npm package's `Package.swift` takes the newest
  2.x from 2.2.35 (beta package) or 2.1.51 (public package)
- Native masking rules do not reach the page: the SDK sees the WebView as one
  control. Use `screenCaptureEnabled: false` to keep page content out of
  captures
- With Swift Package Manager the app needs a Run Script build phase that copies
  `ConnectResources.bundle` into the app root; without it the SDK sends only part
  of its data (see the README)

Our environment for this release:

- Xcode 26.5
- MacOS 26.5
- Capacitor 8, Node 22
- iOS 15.1 or newer (Connect iOS SDK 2.2.35 or newer, the beta build, floating;
  the release build 2.1.51 or newer in the public package, or with
  `useRelease: true` on CocoaPods)
- Android minSdk 26, SDK 36 (Connect Android SDK 11.1.17)
